  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="074 ontology-r1 review K1/K3 (2026-10-17)">Recording billing Decision Records through BillingDecisionRecorder (074) brings two caller obligations.
- (1) Exactly-once. The writer is exactly-once only for retries made one after another; two concurrent calls with one decision id can both write, and a duplicate in an append-only ledger is permanent. So:
  - this task names its serialisation mechanism as an acceptance criterion. Examples: the job claims IIdempotencyService keyed by the decision id; an endpoint does a RowVersion-conditional status update and records only after it succeeds;
  - it adds a test for the concurrent same-id case.
- (2) Decider identity:
  - derive the deciding user from the authenticated principal, never from request input;
  - check invoice access through the UAC filter before recording;
  - pass no contact id. The recorder looks the contact up itself, as DecisionCommitService does; 074's fix round removes that parameter.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner D-104 (2026-10-17)">Build Pending Spend, kept separate from actual spend. It sums In Review invoices that are NOT held.
- Define the pending status set and the held exclusion once in FinanceBaseValues, next to CountedInvoiceStatuses.
- Compute it on both paths: a stored rollup column on matter and project, written by FinanceRollupService; and a live FinanceBaseValues measure for Thresholds.
- The new columns need §11 answers and an idempotent script under scripts/ebilling/. 053 syncs SpaarkeMaster.
- Tests on both paths:
  - must-fire: a held In Review invoice is not in pending spend; an Approved invoice is not in pending spend;
  - must-not-fire: an unheld In Review invoice is counted.
- Keep the fail-closed reader contract.
- ontology-r1 reviews this part.
- Give §11 answers for both the new FinanceBaseValues measure and the new rollup column. The owner approved the additive column (D-104).
- When the column's logical name and type are fixed, send them to ontology-r1. They add it to PolicyVersionValidator.AuthorableColumns, the PredicateCompiler ranges and the RuleBodyDescriber labels in a follow-up of their own. Until then the column is stored but cannot be used in a Threshold rule, which is safe. Do not edit those ontology files.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner D-102 (2026-10-17), amended by D-103">D-103 (counted spend = Approved, Sent to AP, Paid) makes the exclusion below automatic, since a held invoice is In Review. Do NOT add a held predicate. DO add the must-fire test (a held In Review invoice is excluded from both sum paths, through FinanceRollupService and FinanceBaseValues.ReadAsync) and the must-not-fire test (a released invoice that reaches Approved is counted), and name D-102 in both. Original text, kept for the test intent: A held invoice (blocked submission, awaiting Override / Return) must not count toward spend. The FinanceRollupService counted-spend sum excludes it, which also covers POL-COMMIT-BUDGET and the ontology spend Thresholds that read sprk_totalspendtodate. It starts counting when Override releases it. Represent the hold with the state the Override / Return flow already needs (the originating submission's blocked status). Only if the rollup cannot read that cheaply, use a held flag on the invoice that only the lifecycle service sets and clears (§11 answers required). Required tests: a must-fire test that a held In Review invoice is excluded from the spend total and from the threshold input, and a must-not-fire test that a released invoice is counted. Ship this with 049, not later. There are TWO spend-sum paths and the exclusion covers both:
- (a) FinanceRollupService (~235-248), which writes the stored sprk_totalspendtodate;
- (b) FinanceBaseValues.ReadAsync → BuildGroupedSumFetchXml (~130-175), the live sum the ontology Threshold evaluator uses (SignalEvaluator.cs:233) for derived columns such as budget utilisation.
Rules for the predicate and its tests:
- Define the "held" predicate once in FinanceBaseValues, next to CountedInvoiceStatuses, and use it from both.
- Run the must-fire and must-not-fire tests on BOTH paths, including through FinanceBaseValues.ReadAsync.
- A submission link-entity in the FetchXML is acceptable. It must keep the fail-closed reader contract (~180: an unreadable shape throws, never reads as zero).
- ontology-r1 reviews this part (their hot path); include it in the range sent to them.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner D-101 (2026-10-17)">Every submission that was read creates its invoice and lines in step 6 (all-or-nothing):
- accepted → In Review;
- automatically rejected → created with status Rejected through the lifecycle service (task 014), with findings and the report linked to both the submission and the invoice;
- a blocked internal submission → In Review, held from approval until Override / Return.
A submission that fails before it is read has no invoice. Two consequences:
- If the lifecycle service cannot create an invoice directly in Rejected, extend it there (one owner of status). Do not write statuscode elsewhere.
- The automatic rejection's Decision Record (system decision, null confirmer, `automated:` decider, null gate tier) files under that invoice through WriteBillingDecisionAsync (D-100).</constraint>
    <constraint source="D-89 (owner, 2026-10-16) / 046-078 review">ApprovedTimekeeper findings hold the invoice for review and never reject. Enforce this directly (the rule outcome for ApprovedTimekeeper is never Reject), not only through the confidence cap: ReferenceMissingCap and ReliabilityThreshold are both editable settings, and a cap set at or above the threshold would otherwise let the rule reject. Add a test with the cap raised above the threshold.</constraint>
    <constraint source="D-87 (owner, 2026-10-16)">Every submission already carries its tracking number from creation (044 via the allocator). The job does not allocate; a submission without sprk_submissionsequence is a defect to fail closed on, not a case to repair.</constraint>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §10">Every change under src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api follows .claude/constraints/bff-extensions.md.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-002">This processor is the only code that creates sprk_invoice and its lines for eBilling; totals only through 016; status only through 014; core-ancestor fields through 013's guard; owner through IRecordOwnershipResolver.</constraint>
    <constraint source="FR-20 all-or-nothing">Invoice, lines, approval portion and document link are written in one Dataverse ExecuteTransaction (or, if the executor proves that impossible for these tables, by a compensation path that deletes every row it created before marking Failed). Signals are written after the transaction keyed so a retry never duplicates them; if a Signal write fails, the submission stays Processing and the job retries — re-delivery completes the missing Signals and never creates a second invoice.</constraint>
    <constraint source="NFR-08 idempotency">Step 0 of the handler re-reads the submission: Accepted or Rejected → Success no-op; a linked sprk_invoice → resume at Signals / after-steps only; Processing from a crashed attempt → restart from read. Status transitions Received → Processing → Accepted / Rejected / Failed are persisted at each change (ADR-017).</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-018">The handler checks EbillingOptions.Enabled first; off → submission Failed with reason ebilling.disabled, JobOutcome.Failure without retry storm (poison per the existing handler convention).</constraint>
    <constraint source="FR-21 / FR-26 / I-5">Document-level read failures (invoice.read.unreadable, page_limit, no_invoice) → Rejected with that reason code; invoice.read.service_failed and transient errors → retry, then Failed. Rule-based rejection comes only from 073's decider; AI output alone never rejects. Accepted-with-findings keeps every finding.</constraint>
    <constraint source="D-91 (owner, 2026-10-16)">The firm invoice number was typed at upload and the tracking number set at creation. Compare the number read from the PDF with the typed one (normalised: case, whitespace, punctuation); if they differ, record a finding that holds the invoice for review, citing both values. It never rejects on its own, and the tracking number is never changed.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-015">Logs carry submission id, invoice id, counts, timings, reason codes — no extracted values or narratives.</constraint>
    <constraint source="044 K4 (2026-10-16)">A job whose submission row does not exist is Success, not a retry or failure: 044 compensation deletes the row when the enqueue call reports failure even though Service Bus may have accepted the message. Log it with the submission id.</constraint>
    <constraint source="047/048 review (2026-10-16)">When line coding results are persisted, store each line's CodeSetSource / CodeSetNeedsReview (CodingModels.cs) with it, so a line coded under an AI-suggested (unconfirmed) code set stays flagged for review; never persist the suggested set as a mapping.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ontology-r1, Signal dedupe (option a), 2026-10-18">One Signal per (policy, subject) per evaluation. SignalWriter's dedupe key is policy|subjectEntity|subjectId|episode, and a write to an existing open row only refreshes sprk_lastevaluated and the due date, so a second request with the same key in one run silently drops its reasons. Rules:
      (1) The 072 mapper aggregates before the write. It sends exactly one request per key, with the reason codes sorted, the strongest consequence winning (Reject over Hold over Flag), and the evidence a union of FieldRefs with each reason's text kept separate.
      (2) A withheld Duplicate reason (D-112) keeps the withheld template, and no other merged reason or evidence field may mention the hidden prior.
      (3) Store a reason-set fingerprint (the sorted codes plus the strongest consequence) in the Signal's evidence. On re-evaluation, a different fingerprint closes the open Signal as Superseded and raises episode+1 (the policy-version mechanism, task 031 / D-13). The same fingerprint just refreshes. No reason left means the normal closure.
      (4) The Decision Record fact snapshot lists every merged reason.
      (5) D-115's rates-not-checked finding has its own reason code and is flag-only. It must never be merged into a Reject.
    Required tests: two reasons on one line give one Signal carrying both, with the strongest consequence; a reason added on re-evaluation gives Superseded plus episode+1, and the new Signal carries both; a withheld plus a visible reason leak no hidden detail; a must-fire guard shows the mapper never sends a duplicate key in one evaluation.</constraint>
    <constraint source="072 review fix round (2026-10-18)">Three caller obligations from 072's evaluator and mapper:
- Supply BillingEvaluationRequest.ReceivedOn: the submission's received date (sprk_submission createdon, as a DateOnly). Rule versions in force and the D-111 / D-116 window checks are judged on that date, not on the date the job runs, so a retry or a late re-evaluation gives the same result.
- Own the Signal lifecycle that option (a) needs: compare the reason-set fingerprint with the open Signal's; on a change, close the open one as Superseded and write episode n+1; on the same fingerprint, refresh only. The mapper produces the fingerprint; it does not supersede.
- Read the header currency confidence from HeaderConfidence["Currency"] under exactly that key, and normalise "$" to USD before the currency check. A missing currency, or one read at low confidence, counts as non-USD (flag-only; 072's currency_not_usd template).</constraint>
    <constraint source="074 fix-round adversarial pass (2026-10-18), caller obligations of BillingDecisionRecorder">Callers that record billing decisions through BillingDecisionRecorder must:
- Derive the resolved Signal ids and the policy version id server-side, from the decision's own invoice and lines. Never take them from the request. The writer accepts any existing Signal or policy version (074 review K-2).
- Record before mutating, or run the recorder's reads first. Its refusals that depend on Dataverse reads (unknown policy version, missing Signal, undetermined subject, contact read fault) happen after Preflight, so a caller that changes invoice status first can be left with a changed status and no Decision Record (K-3).
- Serialise decisions per invoice (one at a time per invoice). The recorder's "already recorded" check and its create are not atomic (K-4).
- Build Values the same way on every attempt (same types, same decimal scale), so that an honest retry hashes to the same content (K-5, unless 074's follow-up normalises decimals).</constraint>
    <constraint source="072 part L adversarial passes (2026-10-18)">Two more caller obligations for the evaluation request:
- Build BillingEvaluationRequest.CoreRecord from the same submission read that files the invoice, and file the invoice under that core record. If the submission's core record changed between evaluation and persistence (a re-file is a real operation since the master merge), re-evaluate; never persist findings judged under a different core. Otherwise a finding judged under a secure core could show a prior's detail on an invoice filed under an ordinary one (D-112).
- Pass the allocated tracking number (sprk_submissionsequence, D-87) as BillingEvaluationRequest.TrackingNumber. A null tracking number makes Duplicate findings fall back to the headline text.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ontology-r1 re-check of 072 part E (2026-10-18)">Build the Decision Record snapshot and any stored copy of a billing Signal's reasons from the merged, scrubbed BillingSignalMapping form (the request, sentence and evidence), never from BillingSignalMapping.Findings. In a withheld group, Findings still carries the visible reason's values.</constraint>
    <constraint source="072 part W adversarial review K1 (2026-10-19)">A merged billing Signal's consequence is its version's configured consequence, and its primary message is the first reject-consequence reason in code order. Neither is 073's deciding reason. When a submission is Rejected, choose the Signal's primary reason (sprk_sentence) from SubmissionRejectionRule's deciding findings (Rejects), so the firm sees the reason that actually rejected. When 073 holds every reason, do not present the Signal as a rejection.</constraint>
  </constraints>

